---
// The life of a message between participants (ADR-0013, proposed), as a sequence diagram:
// who registers, who appends, who polls, who wakes. Static inline SVG; see diagram.css.
import './diagram.css'

const lanes = [
	{ x: 90, label: 'ASKER (AGENT)' },
	{ x: 310, label: 'CYNAPSE' },
	{ x: 510, label: 'RUNTIME' },
	{ x: 680, label: 'REVIEWER SESSION' },
]
const steps = [
	{ y: 84, from: 510, to: 310, label: '1. register cyberlegion:role/reviewer' },
	{ y: 128, from: 90, to: 310, label: '2. append to reviewer’s channel' },
	{ y: 172, from: 510, to: 310, label: '3. changes(since)' },
	{ y: 200, from: 310, to: 510, label: 'reviewer’s channel moved', reply: true },
	{ y: 244, from: 510, to: 680, label: '4. wake (doorbell)' },
	{ y: 288, from: 680, to: 310, label: '5. read unread entries', labelX: 410 },
	{ y: 332, from: 680, to: 310, label: '6. reply, parent = the question', labelX: 410 },
	{ y: 376, from: 310, to: 90, label: '7. entry wait returns the reply', reply: true },
]
---

<figure class="cynapse-diagram not-content">
	<svg viewBox="0 0 760 400" role="img" aria-labelledby="flow-title flow-desc">
		<title id="flow-title">The life of a message between participants</title>
		<desc id="flow-desc">
			1. The runtime registers the reviewer participant with cynapse. 2. An asking agent
			appends a question to the reviewer's address channel. 3. The runtime polls cynapse
			for changes and learns the reviewer's channel moved. 4. The runtime wakes the reviewer
			session. 5. The session reads its unread entries. 6. It appends a reply whose parent is
			the question. 7. The asker, waiting on the thread, receives the reply. cynapse never
			calls the runtime or a session.
		</desc>
		<defs>
			<marker id="flow-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
				<path d="M0 0 10 5 0 10z" class="head" />
			</marker>
			<marker id="flow-arrow-muted" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
				<path d="M0 0 10 5 0 10z" class="head muted" />
			</marker>
		</defs>

		{lanes.map((l) => (
			<g>
				<text class="layer" x={l.x} y="30" text-anchor="middle">{l.label}</text>
				<line class="edge dashed" x1={l.x} y1="42" x2={l.x} y2="390" />
			</g>
		))}

		{steps.map((s) => (
			<g>
				<line
					class={s.reply ? 'edge dashed' : 'edge'}
					x1={s.from}
					y1={s.y}
					x2={s.to + (s.to > s.from ? -3 : 3)}
					y2={s.y}
					marker-end={s.reply ? 'url(#flow-arrow-muted)' : 'url(#flow-arrow)'}
				/>
				<text class="edge-label" x={s.labelX ?? (s.from + s.to) / 2} y={s.y - 7} text-anchor="middle">{s.label}</text>
			</g>
		))}
	</svg>
	<figcaption>
		Solid arrows are calls; dashed arrows are what a call returns. cynapse only answers. It
		never calls the runtime or a session, so waking is always the runtime's decision.
		Built (ADR-0013).
	</figcaption>
</figure>
